  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported primary data and stated intentions from only three cities in a single region of Northern Italy.",
      "what": "This study analyzes primary survey data from older adults in three Northern Italian cities to examine how their public transit habits for healthcare and socialization survived the COVID-19 pandemic. The analysis reveals that physical accessibility and cost affordability drive transit use for medical trips, while digital transit services and a lack of neighborhood social spaces increase transit use for social purposes. The findings show a strong correlation between pre-pandemic habits and post-pandemic intentions, suggesting long-term resilience in transit use among older populations.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that deep-seated travel habits in older adults can survive major public health disruptions, indicating that public transit demand for essential trips is likely to bounce back rather than permanently decline."
    }
  },
